{"word": "brook", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "brook", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɹʊk/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɹʉk/", "tags": ["Northern Ireland", "Scotland"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/bɹuːk/", "tags": ["obsolete"]}], "forms": [{"word": "brooks", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "brooking", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "brooked", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "brooked", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive, formal, chiefly in the negative) To bear; endure; support; put up with; tolerate.", "tags": ["formal", "transitive"], "examples": ["brook no refusal", "I will not brook any disobedience.", "I will brook no impertinence."], "quotes": [{"text": "how shall I brook to be the first cause of difference between a father and son, to whom the averted look and the harsh word have been hitherto unknown?", "reference": "1834, L[etitia] E[lizabeth] L[andon], chapter II, in Francesca Carrara. […], volume I, London: Richard Bentley, […], (successor to Henry Colburn), →OCLC, page 22:"}, {"text": "But Sophia's mother was not the woman to brook defiance. After a few moments' vain remonstrance her husband complied. His manner and appearance were suggestive of a satiated sea-lion.", "reference": "1921, Ben Travers, chapter 6, in A Cuckoo in the Nest, Garden City, N.Y.: Doubleday, Page & Company, published 1925, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "After delivering the reply he ordered the annalists, who have charge of the knots, to take note of it and include it in their tradition. By now the Spaniards, who were unable to brook the length of the discourse, had left their places and fallen on the Indians", "reference": "1966, Garcilaso de la Vega, H. V. Livermore, Karen Spalding, Royal Commentaries of the Incas and General History of Peru (Abridged), Hackett Publishing, →ISBN, page 104:"}, {"text": "The norm is submission to the supposed iron laws of technological inevitability that brook no impediment.", "reference": "2018, Shoshana Zuboff, chapter 13, in The Age of Surveillance Capitalism:"}, {"text": "The faith in destiny and moral certainty claimed by would-be liberators brooks no resistance, and to register objections to their devotion is to be seen as the enemy of rightness.", "reference": "2019 May 19, Alex McLevy, “The final Game Of Thrones brings a pensive but simple meditation about stories (newbies)”, in The A.V. Club, archived from the original on 22 May 2019:"}, {"text": "On just the first day of the war, more than 1,300 protesters across Russia, many of them chanting “No to war,” were detained, The Times reported, quoting a rights group. That’s no small number in a country where Putin brooks little dissent.", "reference": "2022 February 25, Thomas L. Friedman, “We Have Never Been Here Before”, in The New York Times, →ISSN:"}], "synonyms": ["abear", "abide", "aby", "accept", "allow", "bear", "bide", "brook", "dree", "endure", "forbear", "go along with", "live with", "pass", "pocket", "pouch", "put up with", "stand", "stick", "stomach", "suffer", "swallow", "take", "take sitting down", "thole", "tolerate", "undergang", "undergo"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive, puristic, otherwise Scotland) To enjoy the use of; make use of; profit by; to use, enjoy, possess, or hold.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Yea, my Lord: how brooks your Grace the ayre, / After your late toſſing on the breaking Seas?", "reference": "1595 December 9 (first known performance), William Shakespeare, “The Life and Death of King Richard the Second”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies: Published According to the True Originall Copies (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act III, scene ii], page 34:"}, {"text": "